{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01CVYRNBD3C2kWiUhJKdYfVu","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//\n2\t// Created by Anfernee Jervis on 7/1/22.\n3\t//\n4\t\n5\t#ifndef TED_BUFFER_H\n6\t#define TED_BUFFER_H\n7\t\n8\t#include \"gap.h\"\n9\t#include <stdio.h>\n10\t\n11\t#define DEFAULT_CAPACITY 100\n12\t#define DEFAULT_GAP_BUF_CAP 100\n13\t\n14\t/*\n15\t * TextBuffer\n16\t * This data structure represents the current buffer of the text editor.\n17\t * It's essentially an array of gap buffers, each gap buffer representing a line in the file.\n18\t *\n19\t * [] -> [contents of line |       |  one]\n20\t * [] -> [contents of line |       |  twp]\n21\t * [] -> [contents of line |       |  three]\n22\t * [] -> [contents of line |       |  four]\n23\t * [] -> [contents of line |       |  five]\n24\t * [] -> [|                              |]    Blank line (with a full buffer)\n25\t * [] -> NULL                                    No line. Signals end of the buffer\n26\t * ...\n27\t * [] -> NULL                                    Last element of the array. Signals end of capacity.\n28\t *\n29\t * Note that blank lines will have a buffer. This can be optimized later (with a performance penalty for inserts)\n30\t * Array slots with a NULL pointer are non-existent lines, and signal the end of a file.\n31\t *\n32\t * Having the rows be an array allows constant lookup so file exploration is cheap.\n33\t * Copying small strings is cheap so gap buffers for lines shouldn't be too expensive.\n34\t *\n35\t * Additionally, this structure will hold details about the current state of the text editor,\n36\t * such as the cursor position (row, col).\n37\t *\n38\t * lines: array of GapBuffers representing the lines in a file.\n39\t * lines_capacity: size of the lines array\n40\t * cursorRow: row of the cursor\n41\t * cursorCol: column of the cursor\n42\t * cursorColMoved: whether the cursorCol changed (by a move operation for example)\n43\t * last_line_loc: the last line in the buffer\n44\t * */\n45\t\n46\ttypedef struct TextBuffer {\n47\t    GapBuffer** lines;\n48\t    int lines_capacity;\n49\t    int cursorRow;\n50\t    int cursorCol;\n51\t    int cursorColMoved;    // if cursorColMoved, a move must be performed on the gap buffer before inserts\n52\t    int last_line_loc;  // Index of the last line (also the number of lines)\n53\t} TextBuffer;\n54\t\n55\t\n56\t/*\n57\t * CreateTextBuffer creates and initializes a new text buffer.\n58\t * lines: The number of lines to support initially\n59\t * line_size: Default initial line size to allocate.\n60\t * Returns a pointer to an initialized TextBuffer or NULL\n61\t * */\n62\tTextBuffer* CreateTextBuffer(int lines, int line_size);\n63\t\n64\t\n65\t/*\n66\t * DestroyTextBuffer deallocates the structures in the TextBuffer, and the TextBuffer itself\n67\t * */\n68\tvoid DestroyTextBuffer(TextBuffer* instance);\n69\t\n70\t/*\n71\t * MoveCursor moves the cursor to row and column given. If the values are out of bounds, it's moved\n72\t * to the closest valid position (e.g. if row is negative, it's moved to row 0)\n73\t *\n74\t * Memory is allocated as needed.\n75\t *\n76\t * Returns 0 on success or MEM_ERROR\n77\t * */\n78\t\n79\tvoid TextBufferMoveCursor(TextBuffer* instance, int row, int col);\n80\t\n81\t\n82\t/*\n83\t * Insert inserts a character at the cursor location. Space is made for the inserted character; it doesn't overwrite\n84\t * a character that might be at the cursor's current position.\n85\t *\n86\t * Returns 0 on success, or MEM_ERROR\n87\t * */\n88\t\n89\tint TextBufferInsert(TextBuffer* instance, char ch);\n90\t\n91\t\n92\t/*\n93\t * Backspace deletes the character that appears before the cursor location. Similar to hitting the backspace button.\n94\t * */\n95\tint TextBufferBackspace(TextBuffer* instance);\n96\t\n97\t\n98\t/*\n99\t * NewLine adds a new line to the buffer and moves the cursor to the start of that new line.\n100\t * Handles the logic of hitting the return key.\n101\t * Only works when at the end of the last line of the fine. Used to allow users to add more lines to the end of\n102\t * the buffer\n103\t *\n104\t * e.g.\n105\t * [] -> [contents of line |       |  one]\n106\t * [] -> [contents {}of line |       |  twp]\n107\t * [] -> [contents of line |       |  three]\n108\t * In the TextBuffer above, {} is the cursor. What happens if Enter is hit at that location?\n109\t * 1. The line needs to be split into two lines\n110\t * 2. The new line needs to be placed immediately after the line that was split.\n111\t * 3. The lines below need to be shifted down once\n112\t *\n113\t * The results of this:\n114\t * [] -> [contents of line |       |  one]\n115\t * [] -> [contents|                  |]\n116\t * [] -> [{}|                  |of line twp]\n117\t * [] -> [contents of line |       |  three]\n118\t *\n119\t * Returns 0 or MEM_ERROR\n120\t * */\n121\tint TextBufferNewLine(TextBuffer* instance);\n122\t\n123\t\n124\t/*\n125\t * Returns an allocated string of the contents of the line at the given index.\n126\t * if index is out of bounds, or there was a memory error, returns NULL\n127\t *\n128\t * */\n129\tchar* TextBufferGetLine(TextBuffer* instance, int row);\n130\t\n131\t\n132\t\n133\t/*\n134\t * Creates a TextBuffer with the contents of the file pointed to by the file pointer given.\n135\t * By default, the gap buffer for each line is double the line size or DEFAULT_GAP_BUF_CAP, whichever is greater\n136\t * If fp is NULL, behaves the same as CreateTextBuffer(DEFAULT_CAPACITY, DEFAULT_GAP_BUF_CAP),\n137\t * returns NULL if there's an error, otherwise an initialized TextBuffer*\n138\t * */\n139\tTextBuffer* CreateTextBufferFromFile(FILE* fp);\n140\t\n141\t#endif //TED_BUFFER_H\n142\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d306adf2-6658-43fb-9f52-62b6fdb12059","uuid":"88e9b21a-b271-48d7-bc09-8eec01946efc","timestamp":"2026-06-03T06:18:46.120Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-MTJ0QP/project/c_src/buffer/buffer.h","content":"//\n// Created by Anfernee Jervis on 7/1/22.\n//\n\n#ifndef TED_BUFFER_H\n#define TED_BUFFER_H\n\n#include \"gap.h\"\n#include <stdio.h>\n\n#define DEFAULT_CAPACITY 100\n#define DEFAULT_GAP_BUF_CAP 100\n\n/*\n * TextBuffer\n * This data structure represents the current buffer of the text editor.\n * It's essentially an array of gap buffers, each gap buffer representing a line in the file.\n *\n * [] -> [contents of line |       |  one]\n * [] -> [contents of line |       |  twp]\n * [] -> [contents of line |       |  three]\n * [] -> [contents of line |       |  four]\n * [] -> [contents of line |       |  five]\n * [] -> [|                              |]    Blank line (with a full buffer)\n * [] -> NULL                                    No line. Signals end of the buffer\n * ...\n * [] -> NULL                                    Last element of the array. Signals end of capacity.\n *\n * Note that blank lines will have a buffer. This can be optimized later (with a performance penalty for inserts)\n * Array slots with a NULL pointer are non-existent lines, and signal the end of a file.\n *\n * Having the rows be an array allows constant lookup so file exploration is cheap.\n * Copying small strings is cheap so gap buffers for lines shouldn't be too expensive.\n *\n * Additionally, this structure will hold details about the current state of the text editor,\n * such as the cursor position (row, col).\n *\n * lines: array of GapBuffers representing the lines in a file.\n * lines_capacity: size of the lines array\n * cursorRow: row of the cursor\n * cursorCol: column of the cursor\n * cursorColMoved: whether the cursorCol changed (by a move operation for example)\n * last_line_loc: the last line in the buffer\n * */\n\ntypedef struct TextBuffer {\n    GapBuffer** lines;\n    int lines_capacity;\n    int cursorRow;\n    int cursorCol;\n    int cursorColMoved;    // if cursorColMoved, a move must be performed on the gap buffer before inserts\n    int last_line_loc;  // Index of the last line (also the number of lines)\n} TextBuffer;\n\n\n/*\n * CreateTextBuffer creates and initializes a new text buffer.\n * lines: The number of lines to support initially\n * line_size: Default initial line size to allocate.\n * Returns a pointer to an initialized TextBuffer or NULL\n * */\nTextBuffer* CreateTextBuffer(int lines, int line_size);\n\n\n/*\n * DestroyTextBuffer deallocates the structures in the TextBuffer, and the TextBuffer itself\n * */\nvoid DestroyTextBuffer(TextBuffer* instance);\n\n/*\n * MoveCursor moves the cursor to row and column given. If the values are out of bounds, it's moved\n * to the closest valid position (e.g. if row is negative, it's moved to row 0)\n *\n * Memory is allocated as needed.\n *\n * Returns 0 on success or MEM_ERROR\n * */\n\nvoid TextBufferMoveCursor(TextBuffer* instance, int row, int col);\n\n\n/*\n * Insert inserts a character at the cursor location. Space is made for the inserted character; it doesn't overwrite\n * a character that might be at the cursor's current position.\n *\n * Returns 0 on success, or MEM_ERROR\n * */\n\nint TextBufferInsert(TextBuffer* instance, char ch);\n\n\n/*\n * Backspace deletes the character that appears before the cursor location. Similar to hitting the backspace button.\n * */\nint TextBufferBackspace(TextBuffer* instance);\n\n\n/*\n * NewLine adds a new line to the buffer and moves the cursor to the start of that new line.\n * Handles the logic of hitting the return key.\n * Only works when at the end of the last line of the fine. Used to allow users to add more lines to the end of\n * the buffer\n *\n * e.g.\n * [] -> [contents of line |       |  one]\n * [] -> [contents {}of line |       |  twp]\n * [] -> [contents of line |       |  three]\n * In the TextBuffer above, {} is the cursor. What happens if Enter is hit at that location?\n * 1. The line needs to be split into two lines\n * 2. The new line needs to be placed immediately after the line that was split.\n * 3. The lines below need to be shifted down once\n *\n * The results of this:\n * [] -> [contents of line |       |  one]\n * [] -> [contents|                  |]\n * [] -> [{}|                  |of line twp]\n * [] -> [contents of line |       |  three]\n *\n * Returns 0 or MEM_ERROR\n * */\nint TextBufferNewLine(TextBuffer* instance);\n\n\n/*\n * Returns an allocated string of the contents of the line at the given index.\n * if index is out of bounds, or there was a memory error, returns NULL\n *\n * */\nchar* TextBufferGetLine(TextBuffer* instance, int row);\n\n\n\n/*\n * Creates a TextBuffer with the contents of the file pointed to by the file pointer given.\n * By default, the gap buffer for each line is double the line size or DEFAULT_GAP_BUF_CAP, whichever is greater\n * If fp is NULL, behaves the same as CreateTextBuffer(DEFAULT_CAPACITY, DEFAULT_GAP_BUF_CAP),\n * returns NULL if there's an error, otherwise an initialized TextBuffer*\n * */\nTextBuffer* CreateTextBufferFromFile(FILE* fp);\n\n#endif //TED_BUFFER_H\n","numLines":142,"startLine":1,"totalLines":142}}}
